From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Feb 12 14:53:41 2002
From: jreed () athena ! appliedtheory ! com
Date: Tue, 12 Feb 2002 14:53:41 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: can't specify partition size in 5.1 install
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653731622203

The consensus was that you can specify the partition size in
the GUI during the 5.1 install, but that if you using a terminal
and doing the text-based install, you must still ctrl-z out to
the UNIX shell, use disklabel and an editor to do the partition,
and then restart the install. Painful. Ick! ACK!!

After struggling with the GUI disk configuration, I must say
that it is incredibly counter-intuitive, compared to Sun's. 
You can configure a Solaris disk without reading anything, just
by filling in fields in the GUI. The main Tru64 Installation
guide does not explain how to manipulate the GUI, and trial
and error was very painful. There seems to be a bit more in
the Advanced Install guide, but it's more on planning, not on
doing the gui. 

In the end we finally set all partitions to 0 size, starting
at the last block of the disk, and then were able to start
with the "a" partition and proceed to size them as we wished.


Thanks to all who replied and commiserated!!!!!!!

Judith Reed

From tru64-unix-managers  Tue Feb 12 14:14:23 2002
From: Don Newcomer <newcomer () dickinson ! edu>
Date: Tue, 12 Feb 2002 14:14:23 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: ACLs
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653731622202

We are using access control lists to govern publishing access to the folders
used by our web server (Tru64 version 4.0G).  As a person is granted
publishing access, the script we wrote modifies each appropriate directory
as follows:

	setacl -a -D -u user:smith:rwx dirname
	setacl -d -u user:smith:rw- dirname

It also modifies each object in the directory as follows:

	setacl -a -u user:smith:rw- filename

This works fine but we recently found a flaw that I can't seem to correct.
All of the directories and files were set to be owned by 'nobody'.  After
receiving a call from a person with publishing access, I checked the ACLs and
they were fine.  I tried to duplicate his problem with 'su' and shell commands
and found this.  If the user owns the file, there's no problem.  However, if
the file (and directory) are owned by 'nobody', here's how it behaves:

	* He can copy the file to a new name
	* He can delete the old file
	* He CAN'T copy the copy over the old file (permission violation)

I'm at a loss to explain this one but it's consistent in other directories as
well.  If it were a directory write problem, he shouldn't be able to delete
any files OR create any new ones.  Any suggestions or explanations would be
_greatly_ appreciated.

